A||||3||liebowa||Towing an '87 GT
Z||000000||liebowa||05-18-99||07:45 AM||||I need to tow the above car for 500 miles.  I contacted U-Haul, and they suggested a tow dolly.  Does anyone have any experience with these.  Are the fascias on the Fiero going to get "trashed" with this type of setup?
Z||000001||theogre||05-18-99||08:16 PM||||if you have a heavy enough tow vehicle... like the equivalent of an F250 or F350 ford truck, use a full flat trailer.<p>If you do use the dolly, consider pulling backwards. the front end doesn't care how far it's towed. the transmission does care, and it might not be pretty. U-haul and others provide a light kit to go on the car, so it doesn't matter.<p>Be carefull to check what state laws cover towed cars if you are towing an unregistered car. Some states have rather queer rules about it. for example: MD used to be able to ticket you for it..... And they would to. even wrecker operators got tickets.... 
Z||000002||theogre||05-18-99||08:21 PM||||oopie.... hit button before i ment too.<p>as for clearance.... it depends on just what dolly they give you. it's going to be tight. the facia may be ok, but you might need to take off any skirts on the low end. especially if you pull backwards.<p>use the flatbed trialer if you can. your draw weight towing with the flat bed will be around 3000-3500#. You will want a largish truck for that, a car or SUV isn't rated above 2000# draw even with the big hitches.
Z||000003||RossT||06-01-99||02:15 AM||||I just towed my first fiero from Pontiac Michigan to Bismarck North Dakota. The fiero was an ultra low mileage (475 miles)<br>88gt (thats another story, if you want to hear about it) I used the standard U-Hual<br>auto transport. The kind you drive your<br>car up onto.  I parked the u-haul on flat<br>ground and had know clearance problems<br>whatsoever. The U-haul weighs 2300lbs<br>and the fiero about 2700lbs I used an 85<br>gm truck with a 305ci and had know problems towing it home. I did instal a transmission cooler in the truck.<p>hope that helps.
